body:
Where available, this risk assessment uses data from the years 2020 to 2024 inclusive. These five years represent the period since publication of the last NRA. The data available on TCSPs is more comprehensive now than it has ever been in the past. The availability of this data has significantly informed the risk assessment process. The LPA NRA has identified where additional data and information will be of benefit, some of which is relevant to the TCSP sector. This is addressed in the recommended actions and reference to the LPA NRA will also be helpful.
There are a number of sources informing this document, with the main ones being:

- basic and beneficial ownership ('BO') information from the Companies Registry;

- annual AML/CFT returns submitted to the FSA;

- annual TCSP statistical returns submitted to the FSA detailing staffing, breaches, complaints, outsourcing, insurance, client assets, financial information, customer base, and asset types;

- data collected from the TCSP sector to support an assessment of the risk relating to IOM legal persons and legal arrangements;

- the FSA's central back-office supervisory system (Atlas), which contains inspection and enforcement data

- data obtained from requests to industry solely for the purpose

of the NRA;

- work of the Financial Crime Partnership for TCSPs

- suspicious activity report data from the Financial Intelligence Unit ('FIU');

- mutual legal assistance ('MLA') and asset recovery data from the International Cooperation and Asset Recovery Team ('ICART');

- data from the Proactive International Money Laundering Investigation Team ('PIMLIT');

- data on prosecutions and convictions from the Attorney General's Chambers.
The assessment has been prepared using informed judgement from the Island's key agencies, government, regulators, and industry representatives who participated in the TCSP working group. The working group comprised representatives from the FSA, FIU, the AML/CFT Policy Office, and a sample group of TCSP entities with differing profiles. Additional workshops were also held to assess cross-border threat at national and sectoral level.
In addition, various externally published documents have been considered, including several NRAs and mutual evaluation reports published for other relevant countries, such as the UK, Guernsey, and Jersey, to consider if any risks may affect the IOM where there are similar types of activities carried on. ■

Both the 2015 and 2020 NRAs identified the rating for the TCSP sector as Medium High risk for ML.
While no critical or urgent shortcomings were identified in the 2020 NRA, a number of vulnerabilities were highlighted for the sector to be cognisant of, including:

- the use of TCSPs to provide nominee shareholder activities within complex corporate structures, obscuring identification of the true beneficial owner;

- supply of services, including corporate vehicles, for use in international trade with the risk that those vehicles could be used to carry out one or more of the components related to trade-based money laundering;

- use of high-value assets, such as property, yachts, and aircraft, by HNWIs and PEPs seeking to invest the proceeds of corruption;

- the misuse of vehicles and structures to evade tax;

- seemingly superfluous complex structures and arrangements needing to be treated with caution and the nature and activities of the customer companies and trusts, and the location of those activities well understood.
The NRA noted the importance of TCSPs being aware of these vulnerabilities and ensuring that full customer due diligence ('CDD') was in place, including source of wealth ('SOW') in line with the AML/CFT Code.
The NRA in 2015 called upon the FSA to:

- (i) examine its use of sanctions across all sectors to ensure that they are used appropriately and on a timely basis;

As part of the inherent vulnerability assessment, an analysis of risk factors (as set out in the World Bank module 7A for designated business, including TCSPs) was undertaken. The sector is rated as having an inherent vulnerability score of 0.75 (Medium High). The main vulnerability factors (inherent and control) are:

- the total value of the sector, with 11% employed and £67 billion of assets under administration (inherent vulnerability);

- a higher-risk customer base profile, including provision of services to foreign PEPs, non-natural persons, and the use of complex or seemingly complex structures (inherent vulnerability);

- the international nature of business, such as the non-face-toface delivery of services, use of introducers, and the use of multi-jurisdictional structures (inherent vulnerability);

- the existence of ML typologies and fraud and tax evasion within the sector (inherent vulnerability);

- the robustness of systems and technology, including emerging risks relating to cyber and digital AI risk (control vulnerability)

Source: FSA

- the people risk - shortage and retention challenges for qualified and experienced AML/CFT compliance staff across all seniority levels; vulnerabilities from outsourcing risk where AML functions might be outsourced to third-party service providers (control vulnerability).

NON-HIGHER-RISK JURISDICTIONS:

- 'List C' is a list specifying countries which are considered to have an AML/CFT regime of approximately equivalent standard to that of the IOM;

- 'List D' - Disclosures within a "Group" List;

- Lists C&D are referred to as 'other' in some tables;

- Lists are published by the Department of Home Affairs and updated whenever changes occur;

- Industry is accustomed to updating their own systems and relevant procedures and controls using these Lists.
